WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.500
Hello everyone and welcome to a video that features a most interesting story.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.870
A story that went viral some 10 years ago.

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:11.519
It's about a tournament. It was a chess tournament played in Vandoeuvre in 2008.

00:00:12.429 --> 00:00:18.208
We will feature one game. It's Christian Bauer, a french Grandmaster against Anna Rudolph.

00:00:18.490 --> 00:00:23.939
This is the event where Anna Rudolph won both her International Master norms and Women Grandmaster norms.

00:00:24.269 --> 00:00:29.069
She started the tournament with a win in round 1 and now she faces the top seed in the tournament,

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:33.080
It's like we said, French Grandmaster Christian Bower.

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:36.580
And it is after this game that this whole story begins.

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:39.260
As it's quite interesting, we'll discuss it a bit later.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:43.410
But first let's check out the game, but even before game we do have a nice photo challenge.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:48.660
It's a nice photo by David Llada, so best of luck to everyone. I will give you a couple of hints.

00:00:48.789 --> 00:00:53.159
It's a very good friend of Anna Rudolph. And also she is Anish Giri's wife.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:57.980
So there we have it, best of luck to everyone, now let's check out this game.

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:02.660
Already from the first move the Grandmaster chooses not an everyday approach.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:05.480
He goes for b4 the Polish Opening.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:11.680
We have e5, the standard reply, and now not going for bishop b2 immediately but rather a3.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:13.880
We have d5.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:20.099
It's the most standard principle there exists -- if your opponent allows you to take control of the center

00:01:20.099 --> 00:01:23.180
then by all means to do take control of the center.

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:25.500
So okay, bishop to b2. Knight to d7.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:28.820
E3. Bishop to d6, simply developing.

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:33.089
C4. D captures. Bishop captures on c4. Knight G to f6.

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:36.180
Knight to f3. And we have castles.

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:41.909
Queen to c2 by Bauer. B6, Anna prepares to develop the light-squared bishop.

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:46.799
D3. Bishop to d7. Knight B d2. We have a6.

00:01:48.759 --> 00:01:54.599
Bower castles. B5. Bishop to b3. And now rook to c8, preparing to push c5.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:59.780
Bauer counters this. He plays d4, keeps control of the c5 square.

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:03.480
E captures on d4. E captures. And now bishop to d5.

00:02:04.119 --> 00:02:11.398
All right. We have bishop captures. Knight captures. And now comes Knight to e4, preparing to grab that Bishop on d6.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:13.320
But we have knight 7 to b6.

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:17.260
And now even though grabbing this bishop might seem like an idea,

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:21.840
knight c5 is also a very nice move and the c5 square is a very nice outpost for the knight.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:24.330
So by capturing the bishop, pawn captures.

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:27.760
Queen to f5, as the queen is attacked by the rook.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:30.100
Queen to f6 and black is perfectly fine here.

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:31.920
Queen captures. Knight captures.

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:39.210
This bishop wouldn't have that much fun with all of these points on dark squares. It would be very hard to activate it.

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:43.049
So after knight 7 to b6, rook A to d1 instead.

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:48.180
C6. We have knight to c5 and here comes rook to a8.

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:50.540
Here Anna decides to defend the a6 pawn.

00:02:50.980 --> 00:03:01.460
It's very important to note this, as this was one of the games where people started being suspicious about her using external help from some engines or supercomputers.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:11.940
This move engine never even mentions it. The top recommendation by engine is actually bishop captures on c5.

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:14.760
Not bishop captures on c5, sorry, knight to c4.

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:18.780
There are a lot of options here. Rook to e8, also a nice engine recommendation.

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:22.289
Bishop captures on c5 is a nice engine recommendation.

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:27.390
But the top engine recommendation is actually knight to c4, a very nice improvement on blacks position.

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:35.640
Where white is not able to capture the a6 pawn because now rook to e8 will come with a double attack against the a3 pawn.

00:03:35.830 --> 00:03:42.210
After Knight goes back, knight captures on a3 and this is now a very beautiful and active position for black.

00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:46.439
On the other hand also bishop captures is an idea.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:51.420
But then after D captures white would have a much better pawn structure here on the queenside.

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:52.079
Knight here.

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:54.900
We can play knight d2. Knight captures. Queen captures.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:57.300
And we would have this very nice pawn chain.

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:01.890
While white's pawns wouldn't be that great on the queenside.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:07.319
White pawns are on the dark squares, but white got rid of his weak dark square Bishop here.

00:04:08.050 --> 00:04:11.790
So it would be an all right position to play for both of them.

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:14.320
But okay, rook to a8 by Anna, defending the a6 pawn.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:15.660
We have rook F to e1.

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:18.800
And now comes queen to c7, pressuring the h2 pawn.

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:24.210
So now you could play something like h3. It's what I would play. The Grandmaster decided to go for g3.

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:28.390
The same idea, preventing this pressure against the h2 pawn.

00:04:28.390 --> 00:04:31.900
But also keeping an eye on this f4 square not allowing the knight to go there.

00:04:32.740 --> 00:04:33.740
We have h6.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:38.590
And now comes knight to h4, preparing to bring the knight over to f5.

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:41.240
We have rook F to d8.

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:46.719
And now comes bishop to c1, with future ideas of maybe sacrificing on h6.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:51.309
Bishop to f8, Anna brings the bishop to help out with the defense on the kingside.

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:52.940
Knight to f5.

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:56.979
And now comes a5, as there are obviously no tricks on the kingside.

00:04:57.440 --> 00:05:03.309
Pawn captures. Rook captures. And now comes queen to e2, preparing to bring the queen over to the kingside to join the attack.

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:09.080
Rook goes back a8. Now comes queen to g4, pinning the g7 pawn.

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:13.360
Now Knight captures on h6 is definitely an idea. Anna moves the King. King to h8.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:17.260
And now comes queen to h4, with some ideas.

00:05:17.260 --> 00:05:21.000
Now there's a triple attack against the h6 pawn so perhaps there are some ideas here.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.009
But Anna says "No, there are not".

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:29.240
Rook to e8 is played, even though the engine's suggestion is king to h7.

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:33.140
The engine would prefer if the king joined the defense of the h6 pawn.

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:41.650
But okay. Here, most likely -- I have no idea -- but the Grandmaster probably went into a deep think as there are so many options to play here.

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:47.460
You have to calculate everything. You have to decide whether knight captures on h6 is even an idea.

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:52.920
if Knight captures on g7 is an idea, so we're just gonna cover some basic sacrifices here.

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:58.179
Knight captures. Pawn captures. Bishop captures. Bishop captures. Queen captures. So does this work for white?

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:03.520
King g8. We have rook captures, for example. Rook captures. And now queen to g5.

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:09.660
If the king goes to the H file, then the queen can of course keep checking him and this would be a draw by repetition.

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:17.340
If the king starts running away -- for example queen to h6 check and king to e7 -- now white can bring another piece into the attack.

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:21.399
Rook to e1 check. King d8. Queen to g5 check.

00:06:21.950 --> 00:06:24.400
Now you have to block this. Knight to e7.

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:33.360
And now comes rook to e6. Most likely the Grandmaster found this move as it doesn't allow black to capture it.

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:38.127
Because knight captures would win the queen and also, it doesn't give black all that many options.

00:06:38.127 --> 00:06:40.300
White has a very nice attack, very nice position.

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:43.720
Black is up a knight, but those knights aren't very harmonious at the moment.

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:47.180
For example king c8, now getting the king out of the way.

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:53.280
Now comes queen to h5, pinning this f7 pawn. If you capture then, of course, you will lose the game.

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:58.160
And after rook to f8, the game continues but the engine gives this as a dead draw.

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:00.039
There is no way for either sides to make progress.

00:07:00.290 --> 00:07:03.820
But I'm sure the Grandmaster spent a lot of time here.

00:07:03.820 --> 00:07:07.750
Another thing aside from this Knight captures here, you could also go knight captures here.

00:07:07.750 --> 00:07:14.169
But it brings us still pretty much same position. Captures, captures, captures. And after king g8 we reach the same position.

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:22.140
Also possible after this rook to e8 move is to simply first capture it and then after captures you can go into the same variation.

00:07:22.140 --> 00:07:27.700
Yet again captures, captures, captures, captures and then we reach queen g5 check where we can go into the same line.

00:07:27.700 --> 00:07:34.120
So although white can get at least a draw here, the Grandmaster decides it's not worth it.

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:38.980
There's not enough, you know fuel to power this attack.

00:07:38.980 --> 00:07:44.530
He goes king to g2, improves the position of his king and waits for Anna's move.

00:07:45.170 --> 00:07:48.009
Anna goes queen to d8, immediately offers a trade of queens.

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:53.950
We have rook captures on e8. Queen captures on e8. And now comes Queen to g4.

00:07:54.350 --> 00:08:00.340
This was basically the last moment in the game where the grandmaster could have gone for this special captures in h6 idea.

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:06.009
And after captures, captures, captures, captures into the same queen to g5 line.

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:09.820
But here he played queen to g4 and now it no longer works.

00:08:09.820 --> 00:08:13.540
Here Anna played Knight to f6, attacking Bauer's queen.

00:08:13.540 --> 00:08:16.509
Queen to f3. And now Knight B to d5.

00:08:17.030 --> 00:08:19.030
We have g4.

00:08:20.120 --> 00:08:28.200
White doesn't really have all that much to push with so he has to play something. h4 g5 definitely an idea.

00:08:28.250 --> 00:08:36.100
Bishop captures on c5. D captures on c5. And now Queen to e4. Here Anna says that her pawn structure is better.

00:08:36.980 --> 00:08:42.159
White has a weak a3 pawn and he has a funny bishop on c1 that has to keep an eye on this pawn.

00:08:42.620 --> 00:08:47.770
It will be a much better position for black. But white doesn't have all that many options.

00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:49.870
H3 is played, defending the pawn.

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:55.800
King to g8, Anna prepares to improve the position of the king. And now queen captures.

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:59.270
Knight captures. And here comes bishop to b2.

00:08:59.270 --> 00:09:05.440
This Bishop to b2, as Bauer will state later in his interview -- and this interview was posted, like,

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:12.460
in every blog and every famous newspapers in the world; even the New York Times published in this interview, but we're gonna discuss that a bit later.

00:09:12.460 --> 00:09:20.220
He puts Bishop to b2 as the losing idea -- not the losing idea, but this will cost him a pawn.

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:24.549
And as they are reaching time trouble now, it will be very hard to defend this.

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:31.300
Instead of this bishop to b2 move, with knight to d4, he says, the position is still playable for white.

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:34.160
But okay, it's not really what we're interested.

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:40.280
Bishop to b2 was played and it's very interesting how Anna will take advantage of this.

00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:43.960
The important thing is the bishop on b2 is undefended. So, let's see what happens here.

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:51.640
Anna has an option here. Does she want a guard the g7 pawn by pushing f6 or perhaps go into some immediate exchanges?

00:09:51.640 --> 00:09:53.640
For example knight captures on c5.

00:09:54.050 --> 00:10:00.370
Bishop captures. Rook captures on a3. Bishop captures on h6. and now starts pushing her B pawn.

00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:08.396
This is possible and might even work but she goes for a different plan.

00:10:08.396 --> 00:10:15.200
She pushes f6, first she defends. And now after knight to d4 is played, going after the c6 pawn,

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:19.060
only now does she play knight captures on c5.

00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:23.760
There's no point in guarding the c6 pawn, for example rook here.

00:10:23.760 --> 00:10:26.860
You simply capture and you've eliminated the defender of the knight on d5.

00:10:26.860 --> 00:10:31.720
Captures, captures, and after knight captures on c5 the material is equal. Pretty much a dead draw.

00:10:32.630 --> 00:10:35.760
So after knight e4, knight captures on c5 is played.

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:37.320
Knight captures on c6.

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:44.620
And now feel free to pause the video here and find the only move that allows Anna to push for advantage in this position.

00:10:44.620 --> 00:10:48.300
It has something to do with the move we already discussed.

00:10:48.620 --> 00:10:52.300
So I will give it a couple of seconds if you want to decide whether to do it or not.

00:10:52.370 --> 00:10:56.229
For those of you who were able to do it, congratulations. You are an excellent endgame player.

00:10:56.230 --> 00:11:00.340
And for those you who just want to enjoy the show, knight to f4 check is the move.

00:11:00.890 --> 00:11:05.830
The idea is after king moves, now comes knight to b3. The square is defended by the other knight.

00:11:05.830 --> 00:11:12.369
And now you see the problem: Attacking the bishop that is undefended up on b2, which is basically the defender of the pawn on a3.

00:11:13.399 --> 00:11:18.740
Regardless of where you move the bishop, black is gonna capture it and then gonna capture the a3 pawn.

00:11:18.740 --> 00:11:24.130
And here, in time trouble Grandmaster Christian Bower was probably not very pleased with himself.

00:11:24.589 --> 00:11:28.449
He could have gone for root to b1 which would make things a lot harder for Anna.

00:11:28.790 --> 00:11:32.080
For example knight captures, rook captures, and rook captures on a3.

00:11:32.810 --> 00:11:35.000
This would come with check after king moves.

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:38.770
At least he would already have a king on e2 and already a rook on b2.

00:11:39.320 --> 00:11:43.459
Not allowing this pawn to even be pushed.

00:11:43.459 --> 00:11:47.019
But here after knight F to d3, bishop to d4 was played.

00:11:47.149 --> 00:11:50.840
And okay. Rook captures on a3, threatening all sorts of discoveries.

00:11:50.840 --> 00:11:53.400
Knight to b2, for example, winning the rook.

00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:57.279
Bishop to e3, now opposing the rook. And now comes b4.

00:11:58.040 --> 00:12:02.440
Rook to b1. Now you have to put the rook in front of the passed pawn.

00:12:02.440 --> 00:12:05.540
B3 is coming. The knight is guarding the b3 square.

00:12:05.540 --> 00:12:11.469
We have knight to b4. And now comes b2, as the knight from d3 is already guarding the b2 pawn.

00:12:11.510 --> 00:12:14.880
So if you look at this position and the position we've discussed here:

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:20.529
The pawn is on b5, the king already on e2, rook here. The pawn can't even be pushed.

00:12:20.990 --> 00:12:26.890
We have this position where the pawn is already on b2 and the white rook is very very unhappy on b1.

00:12:27.079 --> 00:12:32.169
So okay. Knight c2, attacking the rook. Rook to c3 and now knight to d4.

00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:34.810
Here we have king to f7.

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:41.979
Also a very nice moment for us to mention that the engine gives knight e5 check as the absolute best crushing move.

00:12:42.620 --> 00:12:46.630
As king g2 is followed by knight to c4 attacking the bishop.

00:12:46.630 --> 00:12:52.869
You don't really have much to do here. After you play knight e2, attack the rook, here comes knight captures with check.

00:12:53.149 --> 00:12:56.360
Captures and now rook to c2, pinning the knight.

00:12:56.360 --> 00:13:01.600
King moves and now comes knight to b3 and there is no defense against knight to d2.

00:13:01.600 --> 00:13:05.000
Either check, winning the rook, or, if you move the rook or if you move the King,

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:08.110
the knight will simply go there just as well, attack the rook.

00:13:08.110 --> 00:13:12.279
You'll either have to sacrifice the rook or move the rook and then the pawn can be promoted.

00:13:12.589 --> 00:13:14.520
But okay. Anna has a different plan.

00:13:14.520 --> 00:13:21.650
It's time trouble after all. She wants her King from g8 to come all the way to b3. So the king starts his journey.

00:13:21.650 --> 00:13:24.920
King f7. We have king e2. Now comes knight to a4.

00:13:25.740 --> 00:13:27.740
We have king d1.

00:13:27.900 --> 00:13:32.959
Rook to c4. Now comes knight e2 and now the king can come closer into the game.

00:13:33.150 --> 00:13:37.740
King d2. We have knight to b4, guarding all of the squares around the black pawn.

00:13:37.740 --> 00:13:40.040
King d1. And now king to d5.

00:13:40.100 --> 00:13:42.980
We have f3 and now comes knight to a2.

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:50.260
Bishop to d2. We have rook to c7, preparing to bring the king over into the game,

00:13:50.260 --> 00:13:52.960
but also getting the rook away from the king.

00:13:52.960 --> 00:13:57.400
It's time trouble, you never know. You never want your king and rook close to any knights.

00:13:58.230 --> 00:13:59.630
So, okay. H4.

00:13:59.630 --> 00:14:05.090
We have king to c4. Now comes king to c2. But now knight b4, kicking the King back as it's check.

00:14:05.430 --> 00:14:10.879
King d1. King b3. The king is now very close and now there is nothing to do here anymore.

00:14:11.030 --> 00:14:12.920
The king is threatening to come to a2.

00:14:12.920 --> 00:14:16.720
Whatever you play -- knight b4 check, king comes here you have to give up the rook.

00:14:16.720 --> 00:14:22.340
Or if you try something like bishop captures, the king will capture and still there's nothing to do here.

00:14:22.340 --> 00:14:25.249
The rook is cutting off the king from the C file. You will simply

00:14:25.950 --> 00:14:28.340
Play king b3, approach the rook, and it's all over.

00:14:28.710 --> 00:14:32.860
So here Grandmaster played knight to c1 check, but it doesn't help.

00:14:32.860 --> 00:14:36.500
We have rook captures, rook captures, pawn captures with check.

00:14:36.500 --> 00:14:38.719
King captures and now knight to c3.

00:14:39.630 --> 00:14:44.780
It's a full piece up, so this is completely winning. But the grandmaster decided to play a couple of more moves.

00:14:45.120 --> 00:14:48.229
Bishop e3 was played. Knight C d5. Bishop d2.

00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:51.260
We have king to c4 and now g5.

00:14:51.260 --> 00:14:58.100
As it is two knights -- it's not a knight and a bishop -- so there is a remote possibility that something like this could happen.

00:14:58.100 --> 00:15:00.440
For example bishop captures. G captures.

00:15:00.440 --> 00:15:07.280
And after f4 -- for example if black knight wasn't there able to capture the pawn -- then g5.

00:15:07.280 --> 00:15:16.400
And then if black actually decided to exchange all of the pawns then, after you get a position like this then it doesn't matter that black is up two pieces.

00:15:16.400 --> 00:15:18.080
You will not be able to win this game.

00:15:18.080 --> 00:15:25.159
Even if you can stop the pawn, two knights and the king are not able to deliver checkmate to a king.

00:15:25.160 --> 00:15:37.689
It's a tempo idea but it is possible in some lines where white actually has an extra pawn, but I don't think this is even close to such a position.

00:15:37.689 --> 00:15:42.160
But okay,perhaps that's why he decided to play a couple of more moves.

00:15:42.160 --> 00:15:45.393
g5 was played. H captures. We have pawn captures.

00:15:45.393 --> 00:15:49.680
And now f5, simply putting a pawn on a light square, not allowing any further exchanges.

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:50.548
We have king to d1.

00:15:51.339 --> 00:15:57.339
King to d3 and now bishop to c1. Knight to c2 was played. Bishop to b2, attacking the g7 pawn.

00:15:57.339 --> 00:16:02.849
And here, on move 65 as Anna pushed g6, placing both of her pawns on light squares.

00:16:03.549 --> 00:16:07.649
Finally French Grandmaster Christian Bauer decided to resign the game.

00:16:08.319 --> 00:16:14.368
So yeah. A very nice game and a brilliant victory against a grandmaster who was at the time over

00:16:14.439 --> 00:16:20.639
400 Elo points higher rated than Anna Rudolph. So definitely a nice scalp to take home with you.

00:16:21.220 --> 00:16:27.628
It's interesting that after this win Anna achieved two more victories. So after four runs, she was four out of four.

00:16:28.209 --> 00:16:33.388
Here I prepared a photo. This is a photo of her and Bauer playing from this tournament.

00:16:33.389 --> 00:16:38.969
It's not a very high quality photo but still, if we have a photo we should definitely show it.

00:16:39.970 --> 00:16:45.940
And here we have a part of an interview with Anna. I will put all of the links in the description below.

00:16:45.940 --> 00:16:54.120
As always you will be able to find an interview with Anna, an interview with Christian Bauer and an interview with the organizers, an article in New York Times.

00:16:54.120 --> 00:17:02.320
So if you're interested in the story a bit more, do check it out. But, to tell you a bit more...

00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:09.240
Here they say "Another participant in the Vandoeuvre Open, Marie Bovarchenko, hinted us that some players

00:17:09.240 --> 00:17:11.489
were uncomfortable with your strong games?"

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:17.780
She says "Yes, there was a “trio” who just simply couldn’t believe I played by myself and accused me of cheating.

00:17:17.780 --> 00:17:27.320
"The trio by names: International Master Oleg Krivonosov, Grandmaster Vladimir Lazarev and International Master Ilmars Starostits.

00:17:27.320 --> 00:17:30.660
"They don’t really know how I cheat, but they are 'sure' that I cheat.

00:17:30.660 --> 00:17:37.800
"Krivonosov was telling stories about nowadays’ high-technology, and so I heard my lip balm became the most suspicious…"

00:17:37.899 --> 00:17:43.169
"Lip balms secret technology? That sounds ridiculous... Will any small kid believe such a thing?"

00:17:43.779 --> 00:17:52.540
"Yes, ridiculous indeed. I usually went away from the board when it wasn’t my turn to move: I like to go out to the fresh air and also to wash my face.

00:17:52.540 --> 00:17:58.720
"Moreover, I played most of my games on the stage, broadcasted, so I didn’t have to sit at the board to see my position.

00:17:58.720 --> 00:18:03.500
"And yes, the lip balm was always with me. Kids, beware, Rybka is in it!"

00:18:05.400 --> 00:18:14.520
After achieving four wins in row, she faced Grandmaster Lazarev and after only 11 moves the grandmaster offered her a draw which she accepted.

00:18:14.740 --> 00:18:21.720
So as Christian Bauer said for his interview for the New York Times, which you are very welcome to read in the description below,

00:18:21.990 --> 00:18:28.480
He said that he actually believes that the other players were convinced that she was cheating because

00:18:28.480 --> 00:18:35.239
first 400 lower-rated opponent than christian power beats christian bauer in the second round and continues winning,

00:18:35.670 --> 00:18:40.070
then the Grandmaster Lazarev was obviously too scared to play against her.

00:18:40.410 --> 00:18:45.240
He thought she might be using an engine so he offered an early draw on move 11.

00:18:45.240 --> 00:18:49.639
But then the worst thing happened in the final round.

00:18:50.600 --> 00:18:54.880
Her opponent refused to shake her hand before the game even started.

00:18:55.700 --> 00:19:00.360
And before the game even started she had to leave all of her belongings with the judge.

00:19:00.940 --> 00:19:05.000
They inspected her little box with the lip balms and everything.

00:19:05.010 --> 00:19:09.410
So that's very nervous. That will get to you. You had a perfect tournament so far.

00:19:09.930 --> 00:19:14.509
She also played a wonderful last game but in the end she lost that game.

00:19:15.660 --> 00:19:22.489
She also says that her opponent played like an anti engine system where something like b6

00:19:22.489 --> 00:19:25.129
c6 d6 e6 or something like that.

00:19:25.500 --> 00:19:30.260
But yeah, like I said you're more than welcome to read a lot more in the description below.

00:19:30.260 --> 00:19:39.650
And another very important thing is Grandmaster Christian Bower said that he never at one point believed that Anna was cheating.

00:19:39.650 --> 00:19:45.259
He just says that after this critical move, bishop to b2, that

00:19:45.960 --> 00:19:49.900
he didn't go immediately for knight d4, with an attack on the c6 pawn.

00:19:49.900 --> 00:19:54.760
He first decided to go bishop to b2 to improve his attack on the g7 pawn.

00:19:54.760 --> 00:19:59.510
This was his critical mistake and he says that after this Anna played a flawless game.

00:20:00.120 --> 00:20:04.660
And they really had to move fast because it was time trouble and everything there was no way for her to cheat.

00:20:04.660 --> 00:20:12.260
and that he didn't really think that her other games against other players were also played

00:20:12.260 --> 00:20:15.140
with outside help with an engine or a supercomputer.

00:20:16.090 --> 00:20:26.280
But that it's very hard for some high rated players to come to peace with that they have just lost to a lower-rated player.

00:20:26.280 --> 00:20:31.829
I'm sure you are all used to if you're playing online, you know whenever you beat the stronger player

00:20:32.050 --> 00:20:36.389
you are always a cheater and when someone beats you they are always a cheater, of course.

00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:41.609
So I decided to include also a description of Anna's profile on Twitter.

00:20:42.040 --> 00:20:46.769
There among other things as a chess commentator, TV reporter, freelance entertainer.

00:20:47.890 --> 00:20:51.359
Entertainer is obviously a word it's like an educational entertainer.

00:20:51.540 --> 00:20:59.040
Not sure if it's really a word but in today's time and age definitely should be. "Passionate and loud. Yoga and zumba aficionado, "

00:20:59.040 --> 00:21:01.220
and there we have it, "lipstick maniac".

00:21:01.960 --> 00:21:03.510
"Chess International Master," and "Olympic player".

00:21:03.510 --> 00:21:11.820
So probably she included this in the description so that her future opponents know that when she is using her lip balm or lipstick

00:21:11.820 --> 00:21:16.140
